d["recap"]="An identity is true for every value of its variables; the square and difference-of-squares identities let us expand and factor quickly, and Pascal's triangle gives the coefficients of any power of a binomial."
d["diagnose"]={"items":[
 "\\text{Expand }(x+6)(x+6)",
 "\\text{Expand }(x-5)(x+5)",
 "\\text{Is }(a+b)^{2}=a^{2}+b^{2}\\text{ true for }a=2,\\ b=3\\text{?}"],
 "doneWhen":"All three answers are written without a calculator, and for Question 3 you have tried numbers, not just opinion.",
 "notes":"Answers. Q1: x² + 6x + 6x + 36 = x² + 12x + 36. Q2: x² + 5x − 5x − 25 = x² − 25 (the middle terms cancel). Q3: (2 + 3)² = 25 but 2² + 3² = 13, so the statement is false for these numbers; one counter-example is enough. Verbal misconceptions: Q1 students write x² + 36 (forgetting the two middle products); Q2 students write x² − 10x − 25 or x² + 25; Q3 students say 'it works because squaring is distributive'. Adaptive routing: if >30% miss Q1, expand with a 2 by 2 area grid before slide 5; if >30% miss Q2, show the cancelling middle terms in colour and go to slide 5 slowly; if >30% accept Q3, test two more pairs of numbers aloud and go to slide 6. If all three are secure, move quickly through slide 5 and start the Investigate route early."}
b=[]
b.append({"title":"Squares and the difference of squares",
 "rules":["(a\\pm b)^{2}=a^{2}\\pm2ab+b^{2}","(a+b)(a-b)=a^{2}-b^{2}"],
 "examples":[
  {"q":"(4x+5)^{2}","steps":["(4x)^{2}+2(4x)(5)+5^{2}","16x^{2}+40x+25"]},
  {"q":"(3x-7)(3x+7)","steps":["(3x)^{2}-7^{2}","9x^{2}-49"]}],
 "fig":None,
 "notes":"Worked solutions (HSA.SSE.A.2: use the structure of an expression). An IDENTITY is an equation that is true for every value of its variables, unlike an equation such as x + 2 = 5 which is true for one. Square of a binomial: square the first term, double the product of the two terms, square the last term; the sign of the middle term follows the bracket. Example A: (4x + 5)²: (4x)² = 16x²; 2 × 4x × 5 = 40x; 5² = 25, so 16x² + 40x + 25. Check at x = 1: (4 + 5)² = 81 and 16 + 40 + 25 = 81. Example B: (3x − 7)(3x + 7): the outer and inner products cancel, leaving (3x)² − 7² = 9x² − 49. Check at x = 2: (6 − 7)(6 + 7) = −13 and 36 − 49 = −13. Link to the pre-session: the area grid for (x + 6)² has the same four parts. Verbal misconceptions: 'the square of a sum is the sum of the squares' (missing the 2ab); writing (4x)² as 4x²; the wrong sign on the middle term of a difference; a difference of squares written as (a − b)². Adaptive routing: if >30% write 4x² for (4x)², redo Example A with the area grid; if >30% miss the middle term, ask them to test x = 1 as the check; Gate Q1 routes to this slide."})
b.append({"title":"Proving an identity",
 "rules":["\\text{expand one side until it matches the other}","\\text{a counter-example disproves, a full expansion proves}"],
 "examples":[
  {"q":"\\text{Prove }(a+b)^{2}-(a-b)^{2}=4ab","steps":["(a^{2}+2ab+b^{2})-(a^{2}-2ab+b^{2})","=4ab"]},
  {"q":"83^{2}-17^{2}\\ \\text{without a calculator}","steps":["(83-17)(83+17)","66\\times100=6600"]}],
 "fig":None,
 "whatif":"What if a number such as 83² − 17² is a difference of squares? Predict the quickest route, then check it with a calculator.",
 "whatifM":"a^{2}-b^{2}=(a-b)(a+b)",
 "notes":"Worked solutions (HSA.APR.C.4: prove polynomial identities and use them to describe numerical relationships). To PROVE an identity, start from one side, expand carefully and show it becomes the other side for every value. Example A: expand (a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b² and (a − b)² = a² − 2ab + b². Subtract: the a² and b² terms cancel and 2ab − (−2ab) = 4ab. So the left side equals 4ab, the right side, for all a and b. Testing a few numbers is evidence, not proof. Example B uses the identity numerically: 83² − 17² = (83 − 17)(83 + 17) = 66 × 100 = 6600. Check: 6889 − 289 = 6600. What if (gold strip): 83² − 17² is exactly the difference of squares, so factor, do not square. Verbal misconceptions: 'checking one number proves the identity'; losing the minus sign when subtracting a bracket (−(a² − 2ab + b²) keeps changing every sign); using the identity but then multiplying the numbers the long way. Adaptive routing: if >30% lose the sign when subtracting the bracket, write the minus outside the bracket and distribute it line by line; if >30% say one number proves it, run a counter-example such as a² + b² = (a + b)² at a = 1, b = 1. Gate Q1 and Practice Investigate 1 link here."})
b.append({"title":"Factoring with identities",
 "rules":["a^{2}-b^{2}=(a-b)(a+b)","a^{2}\\pm2ab+b^{2}=(a\\pm b)^{2}"],
 "examples":[
  {"q":"49x^{2}-36y^{2}","steps":["(7x)^{2}-(6y)^{2}","(7x-6y)(7x+6y)"]},
  {"q":"9x^{2}-30x+25","steps":["(3x)^{2}-2(3x)(5)+5^{2}","(3x-5)^{2}"]}],
 "fig":None,
 "notes":"Worked solutions. Factoring runs the identities backwards. Example A: 49x² = (7x)² and 36y² = (6y)², two perfect squares with a minus between them, so (7x − 6y)(7x + 6y). Check by expanding: 49x² + 42xy − 42xy − 36y² ✓. Example B: 9x² = (3x)² and 25 = 5²; the middle term 30x equals 2 × 3x × 5, and it is negative, so the square is (3x − 5)². Check at x = 1: 9 − 30 + 25 = 4 and (3 − 5)² = 4 ✓. Test for a perfect-square trinomial: first and last terms are squares and the middle term is exactly twice the product of their roots. Verbal misconceptions: factoring a sum of squares, 49x² + 36y², as (7x + 6y)² (it does not factor over the real numbers this way); forgetting the 2 in the middle-term test; choosing (3x + 5)² when the middle term is negative. Adaptive routing: if >30% factor a sum of squares, test (x + 1)² against x² + 1 at x = 1; if >30% miss the sign, ask what the middle term of (3x − 5)² is before choosing. Gate Q2 routes to this slide."})
b.append({"title":"Pascal's triangle and the binomial theorem",
 "rules":["(x+y)^{n}=\\sum_{k=0}^{n}\\binom{n}{k}x^{n-k}y^{k}","\\text{row }3:\\ 1,\\ 3,\\ 3,\\ 1\\qquad\\text{row }4:\\ 1,\\ 4,\\ 6,\\ 4,\\ 1"],
 "examples":[
  {"q":"(2x+1)^{3}","steps":["1(2x)^{3}+3(2x)^{2}(1)+3(2x)(1)^{2}+1","8x^{3}+12x^{2}+6x+1"]},
  {"q":"(x-4)^{3}","steps":["x^{3}+3x^{2}(-4)+3x(-4)^{2}+(-4)^{3}","x^{3}-12x^{2}+48x-64"]}],
 "fig":None,
 "whatif":"What if the power is 5 and the second term is 2, as in (x + 2)⁵? Predict the first two coefficients using row 5, then expand fully.",
 "whatifM":"1,\\ 5,\\ 10,\\ 10,\\ 5,\\ 1",
 "notes":"Worked solutions ((+)HSA.APR.C.5). Pascal's triangle: each number is the sum of the two above it; row n lists the BINOMIAL COEFFICIENTS for (x + y)ⁿ (row 0 is 1, row 1 is 1 1, row 2 is 1 2 1, row 3 is 1 3 3 1, row 4 is 1 4 6 4 1, row 5 is 1 5 10 10 5 1). The binomial theorem: the powers of x go down and the powers of y go up, and each term is coefficient × x^(n−k) × y^k. Example A: (2x + 1)³ uses row 3: (2x)³ = 8x³; 3 × (2x)² × 1 = 12x²; 3 × 2x × 1 = 6x; 1. Result 8x³ + 12x² + 6x + 1. Check at x = 1: 3³ = 27 and 8 + 12 + 6 + 1 = 27 ✓. Example B: put y = −4: the signs alternate: x³ − 12x² + 48x − 64. Check at x = 1: (−3)³ = −27 and 1 − 12 + 48 − 64 = −27 ✓. What if (gold strip): row 5 is 1 5 10 10 5 1 and y = 2: x⁵ + 10x⁴ + 40x³ + 80x² + 80x + 32. Check at x = 1: 3⁵ = 243 and 1 + 10 + 40 + 80 + 80 + 32 = 243 ✓. Verbal misconceptions: forgetting to raise the coefficient 2 in (2x) to the power; losing the alternating signs; using the wrong row (row n, not row n + 1, when counting from 0). Adaptive routing: if >30% mis-square the 2x, write (2x)² = 4x² on the board; if >30% lose the signs, put the negative number in brackets before powering. Gate Q3 routes to this slide."})
