d["practice"]={"practice":{"items":["\\text{Expand }(5x-3)^{2}","\\text{Factor }4x^{2}-121"],
  "done":"Done when: the middle term 2ab is written with the correct sign, the difference of squares has no middle term, and each answer is checked with a number."},
 "apply":{"items":["\\begin{array}{c}\\text{Field of side }(x+3)\\text{ m.}\\\\ \\text{Expand its area; find it when }x=17.\\end{array}","\\text{Use an identity: }\\ 102\\times98"],
  "done":"Done when: the identity you used is named, every step stays balanced, and each answer says what it means."},
 "investigate":{"items":["\\text{Prove }(a+b)^{2}+(a-b)^{2}=2a^{2}+2b^{2}","\\begin{array}{c}\\text{Show that }n^{2}+(n+1)^{2}+(n(n+1))^{2}\\\\ \\text{is a perfect square.}\\end{array}"],
  "done":"Done when: your argument starts from a full expansion, every sign is tracked, and the final line says why it works for all values."},
 "notes":"Answers. Practice 1: (5x − 3)² = 25x² − 30x + 9. Practice 2: 4x² − 121 = (2x − 11)(2x + 11). Apply 1 (a model football pitch for a Jeddah school, model values): area = (x + 3)² = x² + 6x + 9; at x = 17 the side is 20 m and the area is 400 m²; check 289 + 102 + 9 = 400. Apply 2: 102 × 98 = (100 + 2)(100 − 2) = 10000 − 4 = 9996. Investigate 1: (a² + 2ab + b²) + (a² − 2ab + b²) = 2a² + 2b² (the 2ab terms cancel). Investigate 2: n² + (n + 1)² + n²(n + 1)² = n² + n² + 2n + 1 + n⁴ + 2n³ + n² = n⁴ + 2n³ + 3n² + 2n + 1 = (n² + n + 1)²; check n = 2: 4 + 9 + 36 = 49 = 7². Verbal misconceptions: Practice 1 writing 25x² + 9 or the sign of 30x wrong; Practice 2 writing (2x − 11)² ; Apply 1 stopping at x² + 9; Apply 2 multiplying 102 × 98 the long way and slipping; Investigate 1 stopping after expanding without simplifying; Investigate 2 assuming it is true after one test. Adaptive routing: if >30% miss Practice 1, route to slide 5; Practice 2, slide 7; Apply 1, slide 5; Apply 2, slide 6; Investigate 1 and 2, slide 6. Students who finish Apply early move to Investigate."}
d["compare"]={"title":"Which route? Judge the claim",
 "routes":[{"name":"MULTIPLY OUT","m":"(x+2)^{3}=x^{3}+6x^{2}+12x+8","pro":"Needs nothing new.","con":"Long and slip-prone for higher powers."},
  {"name":"PASCAL'S TRIANGLE","m":"1,\\ 3,\\ 3,\\ 1","pro":"Fast for a small power.","con":"You must build the whole row first."},
  {"name":"BINOMIAL THEOREM","m":"\\dbinom{3}{k}x^{3-k}2^{k}","pro":"Works for any power.","con":"Easy to lose a power of 2."}],
 "claim":"(a + b)³ = a³ + b³ for all numbers a and b.",
 "assume":"Did you test numbers where neither a nor b is 0?",
 "spot":{"title":"steal this shortcut","text":"Row n has n + 1 numbers and reads the same backwards, so you only need to compute half of it.","m":"1,\\ 4,\\ 6,\\ 4,\\ 1"},
 "whatif":"What if the second term is negative, as in (x − 2)³? Predict which signs alternate, then test with x = 1.",
 "whatifM":"(x-2)^{3}",
 "notes":"Compare and judge (3 min). Worked solutions. (x + 2)³ three ways. Multiply out: (x + 2)(x² + 4x + 4) = x³ + 4x² + 4x + 2x² + 8x + 8 = x³ + 6x² + 12x + 8. Pascal row 3: 1 3 3 1 gives x³ + 3(2)x² + 3(4)x + 8 = x³ + 6x² + 12x + 8. Binomial theorem: coefficient of x^(3−k) is C(3,k) × 2^k: 1, 3 × 2 = 6, 3 × 4 = 12, 8. All agree. Claim: FALSE. Take a = b = 1: (1 + 1)³ = 8 but 1³ + 1³ = 2. One pair of numbers is enough to disprove. The missing terms are 3a²b + 3ab². Spotlight: row 4 is 1 4 6 4 1, symmetric. What if (gold box): (x − 2)³ = x³ − 6x² + 12x − 8; the signs alternate because 2 is raised to an odd or even power with a minus sign. Check at x = 1: (−1)³ = −1 and 1 − 6 + 12 − 8 = −1 ✓. Verbal misconceptions: 'a cube of a sum splits into two cubes'; 'Pascal is only for adding'; losing the sign for y = −2. Adaptive routing: if >30% mark the claim TRUE, have them evaluate at a = 2, b = 1; if teams finish early, ask which route they would use for (x + 2)⁸ and why."}
d["production"]={"title":"Date orchard and pond","context":"A model date-palm orchard in Al-Ahsa (model values only, not real data) is a square of side (x + 5) m. A square irrigation pond of side (x − 5) m sits in its middle, and the rest of the orchard is planted with palms.",
 "given":["\\text{orchard side }(x+5)\\quad\\text{pond side }(x-5)","\\text{planted area}=(x+5)^{2}-(x-5)^{2}"],
 "tasks":["Expand the area of the orchard and the area of the pond. Subtract to find the planted area as a simple polynomial.","Show the same result using the difference of squares. Find the planted area when x = 40.","The planted area is 1200 m². Find x, the orchard side and the pond side."],
 "doneWhen":["The orchard area is x² + 10x + 25 and the pond area is x² − 10x + 25.","The planted area 20x is found two ways and is 800 m² for x = 40.","The equation 20x = 1200 gives x = 60 with units.","The orchard side 65 m and pond side 55 m are checked: 65² − 55² = 1200."],
 "hint":"Hint for solving: (x + 5)² − (x − 5)² is a difference of squares with a = x + 5 and b = x − 5.",
 "whatif":"What if the pond side is (x − 3) instead of (x − 5)? Predict whether the planted area is still a multiple of x, then find it.",
 "notes":"Model values only, not real data. Task 1: (x + 5)² = x² + 10x + 25; (x − 5)² = x² − 10x + 25. Planted area = (x² + 10x + 25) − (x² − 10x + 25) = 20x. Task 2: difference of squares with a = x + 5, b = x − 5: (a − b)(a + b) = (10)(2x) = 20x. For x = 40: 20 × 40 = 800 m²; check: 45² − 35² = 2025 − 1225 = 800 ✓. Task 3: 20x = 1200 so x = 60; orchard side 65 m, pond side 55 m; check 4225 − 3025 = 1200 ✓. What if (gold box): a = x + 5, b = x − 3: a − b = 8, a + b = 2x + 2, so the area is 16x + 16, which is not a multiple of x. Verbal misconceptions: expanding (x − 5)² as x² − 25; subtracting only the first terms; forgetting the units; answering x = 60 m as the orchard side. Adaptive routing: if >30% cannot start Task 1, return to slide 5 Example A; if >30% miss Task 2, slide 6 Example B; if >30% miss Task 3, ask them to write 20x = 1200 and solve it. Early finishers: the planted area when the pond side is (x − 2)."}
